Cliff Browning (cliff@ealpha1.eng.fsu.edu) wrote:

: Under osf1 how do you do automated backups?  In the sysadmin guide it says
: that you can use scripts and cron, but it doesn't give any examples or go 
: beyond that.

Do you know Amanda ?

This is a backup system that lets you dump several filesystems from
several "slave" workstations on a single "master" host (the dumps are
done in "parallel", the dumpfiles going on a hard-disk before being
taped).

You play with a pool of tapes, Amanda runs once a day and use only one
tape by day (you must have a large capacity tape, i.e. DAT or 8mm).

Amanda has a builtin scheduler : before dumping, it asks the slaves to
know how many data they have to dump for the different levels of dump
and then it "calculates" the levels to use for each filesystems in
respect of two conditions :

   1) all the partitions must be fully of incrementally dumped at each run

   2) there must be (equally distanced) complete dumps of each
      filesystems on the pool of tapes.

The datas that are wrotten on the tapes are restorable with amanda or
with the STANDARD commands "mt", "dd", "uncompress" and "restore"

Amanda is freeware, not as "smooth" as some commercial stuff, but really
useful (usable ?).

We use it to backup 50 filesystems from 10 hosts each night in 3 hours
(4 Go resulting in 2 Go compressed).

Get a look at cs.umd.edu:/pub/amanda/* (I STRONGLY suggest to look at
the archive of the mailing-list before trying to install, all
answers being in those mails : cs.umd.edu:/pub/amanda/maillist-archives/*)

I have a patched version for DEC OSF :
ftp.univ-lille1.fr:/pub/unix/systeme/amanda-2.1.1-decosf1.tar.Z)

Mail me if you have any question.

Regards,

Eric.

