Xref: bga.com comp.sys.dec:5247 comp.unix.osf.osf1:3090

We have a DEC Alpha 3000/400 running OSF1 v2 real-time kernel in 64M real memory
and 128M swap and are using Fortran programs to manipulate large data arrays. We
have come across problems with the code using up large amounts of virtual memory
as well as real memory. We have put the swap area into over-commitment mode in
the hope this may help, however it has not. To illustrate the problem we compiled
the following small program -

program	memory

integer*4	i_temp
real*4		r_temp

do i_temp = 1, 1000000
	r_temp = r_temp + i_temp
end do

end

We then ran this code and performed a `ps -o VFMT` command and got the following

  PID  TT   S	    TIME     SL  PAGEIN      VSZ    RSS   %CPU   %MEM  COMMAND
1032   ??   I    00:00:06  22457     91    1.77M    48K    0.0    0.1   sh /usr/lib/X1
1033   ??   I    00.00.98    785    746    7.63M   280K    0.0    0.4   dxsession
1034   ??   I    00.00.03  22455     57    1.79M     0K    0.0    0.0   sh -c /usr/bin
1038   ??   S    00.05.99      1    722    8.46M   656K    0.0    1.0   /usr/bin/X11/m
2059   ??   I    00.01.18   1109    491    7.52M    64K    0.0    0.1   /usr/bin/X11/d
1808   p1   S    00.00.38      2    384    2.02M   192K    0.0    0.3   - (csh)
2111   p1   R +  00.01.63      0     76    2.27M   264K   38.0    0.4   memory
1069   p3   I    00.00.42   7839    336    2.02M    24K    0.0    0.0   - (csh)

Now, according to this the Fortran program "memory" is using 2.27M of virtual memory.
Why ??

