I don't think you really need disktab entries; when you do a disklabel, if it
doesn't find a disktab entry it'll ask the drive for its own geometry (this is
a very simple SCSI command, I really don't know why other systems (save
NEXTSTEP) don't do it).

For instance, if I wanted to put a disklabel on a raid disk set, I'd just do
something like:

# disklabel -rw /dev/rre0c swxcr

(with swxcr being the RAID device name thingie)

It'll figure out the correct number and put 'em in.  This worked on a Maxtor
P17-S on a DEC 3000/400 as well.

OSF/1 1.3a and 2.0b, I imagine it holds true for all OSF/1 > 1.2 at least.

As to why the original poster (pauls@locust.cic.net (Paul Southworth)) never
saw the disk, you have to MAKEDEV the correct devices *and* add the entry in
the kernel config file for it to be seen on bootup.  The disk will be seen if
the device files are there after boot, you just won't see anything about it
until you use it.

Also, the 'scu' utility can be used to see if a disk can be talked to:

# scu
> set nexus bus X target Y lun Z
> scan edt
> show dev

[If there's no device there as far as the SCSI driver is concerned, you won't
get past the 'set nexus' command]

