Well, I use it all the time (as I'm responsible for dbx here)
and it does work pretty well.  Not to say that we haven't
run into a few bugs from time to time, but who hasn't ;-)

Here's a test program that should illustrate briefly how
to set things up.  The presumptions here are that the
program is invoked with the pid of a currently running
process, and that the process is owned by the invoker,
or the invoker has su access.  Oh yeah, /proc has to be
mounted, else this don't work at all.  Oops, now I see
I mentioned that in the test itself.  Silly me.


#include <stdio.h>
#include <fcntl.h>
#include <sys/proc.h>
#include <sys/procfs.h>
#include <sys/types.h>
#include <sys/ioctl.h>

main (argc, argv)
int argc;
char **argv;
{
  /* assume that /proc is mounted and user specified the pid of the 
   * running process.
   */

        char   pidname[32];
        int    fd;
        struct prstatus prstat;

        sprintf(pidname, "/proc/%s", argv[1]);
        fd = open(pidname, O_RDONLY | O_NDELAY);
        ioctl(fd, PIOCSTATUS, &prstat);
        printf("The pc at the time of the sample is 0x%lx\n", 
               prstat.pr_reg.regs[31]);
        close(fd);
        exit(0);
}


PeterT


