I'm posting this for a friend that does not have netnews access. If you
have any insight into this problem he can be reached at the email 
address listed at the end of the message.

Dec customer support has acknowledged the existence of this problem but 
as yet have not been able to provide a workaround or a patch.

------------------------------------------------

We have been tracking a strange problem in the Xdec X-server, running under
OSF version 2.0.  The program we are working on is unusual in that it 
creates many large pixmaps.  The problem is that as we create more and more
pixmaps, the server begins to grow out of proportion to the incremental 
space we are asking for.  After a while, it becomes almost exponential, and
we quickly run out of memory and swap space.  We do not observe this 
problem under OSF version 1.3 or before.  In all cases, both OSF 1.3 and 
2.0, we have tested this on a 3000/500 and a 3000/300LX: same results.

A further complication is that the problem does not seem to occur until
after a substantial number of pixmaps have been created.  Thus, it is 
likely that few (if any) developers have observed the problem!  Also,
various and unpredictable changes in the code can make the problem 
disappear, only to return when some other seemingly minor modification is
made.

To illustrate the problem, run an X client including this code:

    jlimit = 320;
    for (j=0; j<jlimit; j++)
        {
        p = XCreatePixmap(display, XtWindow(appshell), 512, 512, 8);
        XSync(display,False);
        ximage->data[10] = j;   /*useless: but prevents 
                                  possible optimization*/
        XPutImage(display, p, gc, ximage, 0, 0, 0, 0, 500, 500);
        XSync(display,False);
        }

Rather than growing by 80 Mbytes, the server process size grows by 
125 M-bytes. When these Pixmaps are destroyed -- or when the client process
terminates -- the server process does not decrease in size. Sometimes, 
afterwards the server cannot make connections using shared memory.
(Get SMT-WARNING ... using Unix-domain socket.)

Setting jlimit down to 80 will result in no problems.  The server will grow
by about the expectred 20 M-bytes, and after the client process is 
terminated, all of this memory is returned to the system, as expected.
Adding some delays sometimes causes the problem to disappear, changing the 
order of steps in the code sometimes helps, changing the size of the 
pixmaps (e.g. to 1024 x 1024 x 8) sometimes helps, but we have not been
able to find a consistent set of rules, much less a set we could live width!
The one consistent relationship seems to be that if the server grows only as
expected, it returns the client-requested memory to OSF when the client is
terminated, but if the server is "pushed" into the "bad" state (allocating 
more memory than is needed), it will not return any memory (except maybe a
megabyte or so).

Under OSF 1.3, the excessive growth problem does not occur, but we observe 
the well known problem that the server does not return any memory to OSF. 
This is unpleasant, but is one we can live with.

Does anyone have a clue as to what is happening?  I also have a complete 
test program that I can E-mail to anyone who wants to poke at it.
It is about 13 K-bytes of c code, so I didn't want to clutter up this 
forum.

