My understanding is that Digital is the only vendor which took the OSF/1
code and created a new product from it - ie DEC wrote a new operating
system from "scratch." Digital calls their product DEC OSF/1

Other vendors chose to take OSF/1 "features" and implement them in their
own already existing products. That I am aware of both IBM and HP chose
this route. 

IBM's "user interface" was the technology selected by OSF to provide the
"user interface" in OSF/1. Consequently, IBM's AIX "looks" like OSF/1
because it is(was) the same code.

HP and other provided the OSF selected technology for other parts, I don't
recall which ones.  I believe that Trans Arc (now a wholy owned subsidiary
of IBM) provided the Advanced File System Technology to which Digital
"added value" in the form of the support utilities (layerd products.)

Contrary to most popular conceptions, OSF/1 is NOT a "common operating system."
Rather, it is a basic collection of "stuff" to which the members of the OSF
consortium are expected to "add value" to be for they "re-sell" it.

Digital's OSF/1 product will not look like IBM's OSF/1 product, which will
not look like HP's OSF/1 product. The expectation of "value added" inherent
in the charter of the consortum assures us that it will not be a single
operating system which will run on anybody's platform. Rather you will have
everybody providing something which will look pretty similar - even though
there are some pretty substantial differences between the implementations.

Unix(tm) is no more a "lingua franka" in computing than COBOL is. In
reality, FORTRAN is probably the closest thing to a truly portable
language/environment existing in the computer world today... still!
