Under OSF 2.0:

Changing the perms of a file that is a symbolic link to another file
causes nasty things to happen ...

	mkdir try
	cd try
	touch file
	ln -s file link
	ls -al
total 6
drwx------   2 stuart   staff        512 Aug 29 18:18 .
drwxr-xr-x  49 stuart   staff       3584 Aug 29 18:18 ..
-rw-------   1 stuart   staff          0 Aug 29 18:18 file
lrwxrwxrwx   1 stuart   staff          4 Aug 29 18:18 link -> file

	chmod g+r link
	ls -la
total 6
drwx------   2 stuart   staff        512 Aug 29 18:18 .
drwxr-xr-x  49 stuart   staff       3584 Aug 29 18:18 ..
-rwxrwxrwx   1 stuart   staff          0 Aug 29 18:18 file
lrwxrwxrwx   1 stuart   staff          4 Aug 29 18:18 link -> file

When determining the new perms for the file, chmod has used the existing
perms from the "link" entry instead of the "file" entry. Can see this by
doing a   chmod g-r link  above instead, which yields:

total 6
drwx------   2 stuart   staff        512 Aug 29 18:25 .
drwxr-xr-x  49 stuart   staff       3584 Aug 29 18:25 ..
-rwx-wxrwx   1 stuart   staff          0 Aug 29 18:26 file
lrwxrwxrwx   1 stuart   staff          4 Aug 29 18:25 link -> file


-SRK


