: >Under OSF 1.3, the excessive growth problem does not occur, but we observe 
: >the well known problem that the server does not return any memory to OSF. 
: >This is unpleasant, but is one we can live with.

: Well, the problem is that free-ing memory does *not* return the memory
: to the system under most versions of UNIX. Therefore memory that has
: been allocated won't be freed until the program terminates.

Actually, the problem is that "malloc" uses the "sbrk" call to extend
memory, and that cannot be undone to free the memory later.  A better
method would be to use "mmap" to add memory segments as needed, but
this would require a different malloc.

I don't know why there isn't a better malloc out there that uses mmap.
Anyone know?

