magill@staff.dccs.upenn.edu (William H. Magill) writes:
>[...]  I believe that Trans Arc (now a wholy owned subsidiary
>of IBM) provided the Advanced File System Technology to which Digital
>"added value" in the form of the support utilities (layerd products.)

This is incorrect.  There's a lot of confusion in this area due to poor
choice of acronyms... let's see if I can get it right...

A network filesystem incorporating many advanced features (global namespace,
redundant servers, authenticated access, etc.) was developed as part of
the Andrew project at Carnegie-Mellon University, which was funded in large
part by IBM.  Originally, it was known as the Andrew File System - AFS for
short.  Transarc Corporation was founded with a large infusion of $$$ from
IBM, with the intent to "productize" AFS.  I believe "AFS" has ceased to
be an acronym for Andrew File System, at least as far as Transarc (and
their trademark) is concerned, but that _is_ what it originally stood for.

(IBM's always had a substantial share in Transarc, but their being a
"wholly owned subsidiary" is news to me... can anyone confirm this?)

Transarc then went on to contribute a Distributed File System (DFS) as
part of the OSF's Distributed Computing Environment (DCE).  DFS can be
seen as a "next generation" AFS, but it differs from AFS in a number of
ways.

Digital's Advanced File System on OSF/1 is NOT related to Transarc's AFS.
It was developed in-house by Digital, and as far as I know does not contain
any technology developed by or contributed to OSF.  Originally, Digital
used the "AFS" acronym, but they've since changed to AdvFS to (try to)
avoid confusion with Transarc's product.  It's certainly not possible
to confuse them technically - AdvFS is a _local_ filesystem (although
AdvFS filesystems can be exported for NFS access, just as UFS filesystems
can), while AFS is strictly a network filesystem.

Hope all of that "alphabet soup" makes some sense...

(All of the TLAs above are trademarks of their respective companies, but
it would take me weeks to get it all correct, so I won't even try... :-)

-Shawn Mamros
