Yup.

|>Does putting /usr on an AdvFS filesystem really work? Why couldn't DEC
|>have made this an option at install time instead of messing around like
|>this? Is it any better under 3.0?
|>

Yes, this does work.  In V3.0, it is an install time option.  The timing of the
introduction of AdvFS in V2.0 didn't allow for the installation to support it.

|>Oh yeah. Is it really corrupt? And if not how do I tell setld its really
|>alright? How do I fool setld into thinking I haven't really removed the 
|>X Fonts and XServer? (This is a fileserver, and I find it really stupid 
|>to install massive subsets I'll never use, but DEC seem to think they
|>know best (As per usual....)
|>

Look in (or send me) /var/adm/smlogs/fverify.log.  One of the places that a subset could become corrupt is during the load of it to the system when the subset verification occurs.  Since you say that a previous setld -i showed the subset "installed", you probably won't find errors listed here - but it is worth checking.

You will also want to execute fverify to determine if the subset is really corrupt.

	cd /; /usr/lbin/fverify < /usr/.smdb/OSFBASE200.inv

See fverify(8) for more information.

The file /usr/.smdb/OSFBASE200.dw is what tells setld that the subset is corrupted.  It may contain information which explains why.  Does it say anything?

As far as the X stuff, they were installed because you have a graphics monitor on the system OR because you selected the subsets.  They would not have been
installed if your system lacked this capability.  If there are specific files you want to get rid of and they are part of the same subset, you might be able to delete the entire subset.

