In Article <33nidn$8aa@dekalb.DC.PeachNet.EDU>
douglas@dekalb.DC.PeachNet.EDU (Douglas Jones) writes:
>Is there a way to mount from the cdrom drive a cdrom with a dos file system?
>We have some cdroms with pictures on them and I was wondering if I could
>mount it on the workstations' cdrom drive. Then I could nfs it over or whatever.

They should mount with no problems.

Most CDROMs are in either ISO 9660 or "High Sierra" format. (These are very
similar). The formats are used for CDROMs on most all systems including OSF/1,
most BSD and SYSV derived Unixes, MSDOS, and, finally, VMS.

They may be mounted using the -t cdfs flag. Be sure to check out the defperm,
noversion and rrip options as one or more may be needed to make the system look
right. (OSF/1 makes all uppercase file names with version numbers a bit hard to
handle, but I believe "noversion" fixes it up.)

