In article <CvAsAB.9tE@world.std.com>, gnewman@world.std.com (Gary Newman) writes:
|> Actually, the problem is that "malloc" uses the "sbrk" call to extend
|> memory, and that cannot be undone to free the memory later.  A better
|> method would be to use "mmap" to add memory segments as needed, but
|> this would require a different malloc.
|> 
|> I don't know why there isn't a better malloc out there that uses mmap.
|> Anyone know?

Actually, "sbrk" can be used to return memory to the system.  Passing a
negative value to "sbrk" reduces the virtual size of the process.  Thus, "free"
can return memory to the system when that memory is at the end of the process's
heap.  The malloc system in DEC OSF/1 V3.0 supports this feature and even
allows applications to tune the behavior.  See the malloc() manpage for more
details if you are interested.

