The Dec X Server provided with OSF/1 2.0 normally returns memory to the 
system as described above and the Dec X Server provided with OSF/1 1.3 
version does not. However, this was not the point of my original article.

The point was this: 

If a large number of images are sent to Dec's OSF/1 2.0 server, it gets
into a state where it is adding exponentially more memory for each image
sent to it.  It eventually starts allocing 2x's the memory required for
each image sent. Further, after it gets into this state it stops
returning memory to the system when the images are released.

This exponential allocation behavior is unique to the server that Dec 
provides with OSF/1 2.0. It does not occur in either the OSF/1 1.3 server
or a server built from the X11R5 source code from MIT.

Dec customer support has acknowledged that the problem exists. They have
been able to reproduce it with the sample program provided in the
original article,  Unfortunately, they have not been able to provide 
a work around or a patch for the problem.

Does anyone have any insight into why the server provided with OSF/1
V2.0 is behaviing this way?  And, more importantly, is there a fix or a
workaround?

