Jim Skoog (skoog@netcom.com) wrote:
:> What is it difference between the files such as dump, fsck,
:> restore, etc. found in /sbin and those in /usr/sbin?

Dear Jim Skoog,

it seems to me that the ones in /sbin are supposed to be part of the
root "/" filesystem and thus to be used as tools in emergency situations,
i.e. when /usr cannot be mounted. Here's why:
> % uname -a  
> OSF1 ventoux V1.3 112 alpha    
> % file /sbin/fsck
> /sbin/fsck:     COFF format alpha demand paged executable stripped - version 3.11-1
> % file /usr/sbin/fsck                                        
> /usr/sbin/fsck: COFF format alpha dynamically linked, demand paged executable stripped - version 3.11-0                                                 
> % mount                                                      
> /dev/rz3a on / type ufs (rw)                                                  
> /dev/rz3g on /usr type ufs (rw)                                          

The binaries in /sbin are statically linked and can thus be run without
the runtime shared libs in other directories.

I hope this is close enough to the truth.
Yours
Torsten
