> as:
> 
> 0,0,1,1,2,2,3,3 
> 
> where the first 0 is used by the original 
> controller and the second 0 would be used by 
> an additional controller. Is there a way to 
> use the second set of slots without buying 
> a second SCSI controller? If not then why?
> If I need to buy another controller to do
> this which one do you suggest?
> -- 

I installed a 2100 in June with 3 disks from the factory 
with the intention of adding other drives I already had in 
a storage works cabinet. I thought I had the same problem 
with the drive ids being in alternate slots, etc until I 
found that you can configure up  to 8 drives in the storage 
 works array. 

This array is actually a RAID type configuration and you 
can build a variety of RAID device configurations. I found 
that you can configure the drives as what is called JBOD - 
Just A Bunch of Disks. This means that you can configure th 
e array as up to 8 individual drives. You need to use the 
SWXCRMGR software utility to configure this. The 
instructions for doing this are in one of the configuration 
manuals.
It took me a couple of trys to get it set up right but now 
it actually seems pretty simple to do. In the SWXCRMGR 
utility you can either manually define each slot as a 
specific device or run a auto-configure and it will do it 
for you. The devices show up as DRA0-7 (at least on my 
machine).

Hope this helps.


