The examples you cite aren't so suspicious, when you consider what they
have to do:

ps: open /dev/kmem (or perhaps the "files" in /proc) for reading.
{d,}xterm: open and manipulate pseudo-ttys.
dxpause: read from the passwd file or the root-only enhanced security
         database.

It seems to me that ps and dxpause could be made to work setgid instead
of setuid, if appropriate groups were given ownership and read-only
capabilities on /dev/kmem and the enhanced security database.  But there
may be other issues there of which I'm not aware...

