In <940824112139@cavedog.ftp.com> mamros@ftp.com  (Shawn Mamros) writes:

>It seems to me that ps and dxpause could be made to work setgid instead
>of setuid, if appropriate groups were given ownership and read-only
>capabilities on /dev/kmem and the enhanced security database.  But there
>may be other issues there of which I'm not aware...

There is nothing wrong with this method, in fact a number of systems do
it this way. It's just that OSF has chosen not to. In the case of NetBSD
(the system with which I am most familar, above OSF):

-r-xr-sr-x  1 bin   kmem    147456 Aug 23 18:03 /bin/ps
crw-r-----  1 root  kmem    2,   1 Aug  1 01:32 /dev/kmem

ps is setgid only, to the group kmem. notice also that this is the kmem
version of ps, there is also a version of ps which uses the /proc
filesystem. The point of the /proc filesystem is that these processes
need not have -any- priveleges to access them. all subdirectories of
/proc (which are PID numbers) are owned by the owner of the process, and
are mode 555.

fstat, ipcs, lpq, lpr, lprm, modstat, nfsstat, uptime, vmstat, w, wall,
and write are several other examples of this.

It's all moot though, anyway, since OSF does this for a very tiny number
of utilities, and this -is- an OSF newsgroup, so I'll shut up now ;)

Cheers,
Phil
