|> on the alpha write (i.e. no one else can). The other thing that you need to 

This is not correct. If you specify -r=0 you grant the root user on the remote
machine the same access to the file system as the local root user, but this will
not influence other users. It is a requirement, though, that uids must match on
the local and on the remote filesystem for ordinary users to gain access. If
this is not so the access-bits on the exported directory have to be set with
extended rw rights (to group or world).

