Summary: Invitation to register for one or two Seminars at the DECUS Symposium in Canberra
Keywords: DECUS Symposium Seminars E-Mail Windows/NT Teamlinks Security Mobile Client/Server Rdb OSF/1 PATHWORKS OpenVMS Multinet TCP/IP PMDF
Xref: bga.com news.announce.conferences:1587 biz.dec:301 comp.org.decus:3198 comp.os.vms:15045 comp.sys.dec:5003 comp.unix.osf.osf1:2950 comp.unix.ultrix:2756 vmsnet.misc:765


                       DECUS Australia 1994 Symposium

                    National Convention Centre, Canberra
                             August 20th - 25th

                         DECUS Educational Seminars

    If you are planning to attend the DECUS Australia 1994 Symposium, I
    would like you to consider enrolling in one or even two of our DECUS
    Educational Seminars (DESes).

    The DESes are being held on Saturday August 20th and Sunday August
    21st, to be followed by four days of papers.  No papers will be
    scheduled against any DES so you don't have to worry about missing a
    paper you were particularly keen on attending.

    All DESes are presented by people who are highly qualified to speak on
    their chosen topics.  In particular, the PMDF, Multinet and OpenVMS
    seminars are presented by those products' respective developers.

    DECUS Educational Seminars cost $335 for one seminar or $525 for two.
    The seminar cost includes Session Notes, Lunch and Morning & Afternoon
    Teas.

    Full details including Abstracts & Speaker Biographies can be found in
    the "DECUS Australia 1994 Symposium Registration Kit" mailed out to all
    members earlier this year.  If you haven't received your kit, or you're
    not a DECUS member but you'd like to attend, please contact the DECUS
    Office A.S.A.P. during normal business hours on (02) 561 5274, Fax (02)
    561 5596.


    Information is available on-line from the following sources:

    1. The mail server at vsm.com.au can provide several files containing
    information about the Symposium and Seminars.

    Send a mail message to mailserv@vsm.com.au with one or more commands of
    the form
    	SEND filespec
    in the body of the message (do not give the message a Subject:).
    The files available are:

    [SYMPOSIUM]MOBILE_SEMINAR.TXT	"Mobile Client/Server Computing:
    					 Issues & Opportunities"
    [SYMPOSIUM]MULTINET_SEMINAR.TXT	"Multinet Configuration, Management
    					 & Troubleshooting"
    [SYMPOSIUM]OSF_SEMINAR.TXT		"OSF Systems Management"
    [SYMPOSIUM]PATHWORKS_SEMINAR.TXT	"PATHWORKS V5.0"
    [SYMPOSIUM]PMDF_SEMINAR.TXT		"E-Mail Integration With PMDF"
    [SYMPOSIUM]RDB_SEMINAR.TXT		"An Overview of DEC Rdb V6.0 New
    					 Features"
    [SYMPOSIUM]SECURITY_SEMINAR.TXT	"Multi-Platform Security Issues,
    					 Policies & Procedures"
    [SYMPOSIUM]TEAMLINKS_SEMINAR.TXT	"Teamlinks - What It Is And How To
    					 Deal With It"
    [SYMPOSIUM]VMS_PERF_SEMINAR.TXT	"OpenVMS Complete Performance
    					 Management"
    [SYMPOSIUM]WINDOWS_NT_SEMINAR.TXT	"Microsoft Windows NT Awareness"

    Also available:
    [SYMPOSIUM]MANAGEMENT.TXT		Management Forum timetable
    [SYMPOSIUM]SEMINAR_SUMMARY.TXT	Dates & Titles of each Seminar
    [SYMPOSIUM]SPEAKERS.TXT		List of Symposium Speakers
    [SYMPOSIUM]TIMETABLE.DOC		Symposium Timetable (DECwrite)
    [SYMPOSIUM]TIMETABLE.PS		Symposium Timetable (PostScript)
    [SYMPOSIUM]TIMETABLE.TXT		Symposium Timetable (ASCII text)


    2. The World Wide Web server at Adelaide University has information
    about the Symposium.  The URL is:
              http://www.adelaide.edu.au/DECUS/Symposium94.html
    This server has details of all DESes in ASCII text format as well as
    the Symposium Timetable in both ASCII text and PostScript formats.


    The four DESes to be held on Saturday are:

    "E-Mail Integration With PMDF"
    Presenter: Kevin Carosso, Innosoft International

         After an overview of the PMDF product, basic condifiguration
         guidelines and using PMDF to exchange mail over the Internet,
         this seminar conventrates on two important recent
         developments in the PMDF product: interconnectivity with
         PC-based electronic mail products, and the PMDF X.500
         Directory Service.


    "Microsoft Windows NT Awarness"
    Presenter: Harry Eleftheriou, Digital Learning Services

         This seminar provides an introduction to the Microsoft
         Windows NT operating system and user environment, its
         relationship to other Microsoft products, and compares
         Windows NT to other PC operating systems.  Also included are
         discussions of Digital's goals for Windows NT, major features
         of Windows NT (including networking, file systems, user and
         kernel subsystems) and other products to support Windows NT.


    "Teamlinks - What Is It And How To Deal With It!"
    Presenter: Robyn Clinch, Dept Human Services & Health

         This seminar gives a practical perspective on the issues to
         be dealt with when installing Teamlinks into an existing
         All-In-One (or similar) environment.  The product is viewed
         through the eyes of Management, Users and Technical Support
         groups and so is suitable for those considering installing
         Teamlinks as well as those who have it running but want to
         learn more.


    "Multi-Platform Security Issues, Policies & Procedures"
    Presenter: Danny Smith, Security Emergency Response Team

         The Security Emergency Response Team (SERT) is a service
         provided by the University of Queensland to coordinate the
         gathering of information on security breaches and potential
         security problems, and then to disseminate that information
         to computer managers and security managers.  This seminar
         covers security issues ranging from the importance of
         security, through examples of security breaches, to
         protection measures.  Also addressed are security policies
         and ways of implementing those policies in a computer network
         which consists of a diverse set of hardware and software,
         including VMS, Ultrix and OSF/1.


    The six seminars to be held on Sunday 21st August are:

    "Mobile Client/Server Computing: Issues & Opportunities"
    Presenter: Larry Cordani, Digital CE (Networks)

         The growing use of portable computers (laptops and notebooks)
         coupled with new Client/Server techniques opens a whole new
         world of possibilities.  This seminar discusses usage models,
         security considerations, remote access, infrastructure,
         disconnected operation and application design.


    "An Overview Of DEC Rdb V6.0 New Features"
    Presenter: Eve Kleiman, Digital Learning Services

         Version V6.0 of DEC Rdb aims to provide improved performance,
         additional SQL features and support for very large databases.
         This seminar covers the issues which database administrators
         and system managers will come across as they prepare for the
         upgrade.


    "OSF Systems Management"
    Presenter: Chris Doney, Digital Learning Services

         This seminar is for those who will be running and responsible
         for a Digital OSF/1 system.  It  includes material covering
         operating system installation, file system management, user
         management and general system administration.  Also covered
         are system monitoring techniques and troubleshooting tips.


    "PATHWORKS V5.0"
    Presenter: Jack Boers, Digital Learning Services

         This seminar provides and overview of the new version of
         PATHWORKS which many sites will be moving toward implemting,
         if they have not done so already.  The seminar looks at new
         features in V5 as well as differences between V4 and V5
         upgrade considerations.  In particular it looks at management
         of a PATHWORKS network from a client PC and the new licensing
         scheme.


    "OpenVMS Complete Performance Management"
    Presenter: Keith Walls, Insoft

         This seminar provides the theory behind management of systems
         performance as well as practical advice on dealing with
         performance changes as a result of workload change and
         growth.  Consideration will be given to such as issues as
         hardware replacement vs. extension of existing hardware
         facilities, and producing a useful capacity plan.


    "MultiNet Configuration, Management, and Troubleshooting"
    Presenter: Trey Garlough, TGV

         This seminar is an in-depth coverage of management issues of
         the Multinet product set.  An overview of the product is
         followed by installation and configuration advice, NFS Client
         and Server issues, security features, and utilities for
         monitoring and troubleshooting.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
        Jeremy Begg		jeremy@vsm.com.au
	DES Coordinator,
	1994 DECUS Australia Symposium, Canberra, Aug 20-25


