In article <32db3t$m4t@news1.hh.ab.com>, tak@cle.ab.com (Teresa A. King) writes:

>Hello,
>
>I'm trying to discover the standard place (if one exists) for installing files.
>In particular I need to know where to install shareable libraries, release notes,
>and sample programs.

The chapter "Creating and Managing Software Product Kits" in the
"Programming Support Tools" document has the information you 
want.  It goes something like:

1.  Put all your kit under /usr/opt (read-only) and/or /var/opt
    (read-write).

2.  Use symbolic links from the "usual" place to point back into
    your kit directory in {usr,var}/opt.  Example:  Manual pages
    like to appear in /usr/man/man#, which is actually /usr/share
    /man/man#.  Put your manual page under /usr/opt/YOURKIT/man.page
    and create a symbolic link in /usr/man/man# back to it.

>  Teresa A. King @ Allen-Bradley Company, Cleveland Ohio

