>get my old terminal z19 working on alpha through a dial up connection.

The termcap entry for h19 from any other UNIX box would probably work
well enough for software that uses termcap.  However, I believe that
most of the DEC OSF/1 software uses terminfo instead of termcap, so
you'll want to find an appropriate .ti file somewhere, or write one
based on a termcap entry (they do place sample .ti files in
/usr/share/lib/terminfo, and the terminfo(4) man page provides some info
on how to translate from termcap to terminfo), then compile it using tic.

*flame on*
You wouldn't have had to go to this much trouble were it not for an
act of typical Digital stupidity.  The original OSF/1 from OSF contained
a full set of termcap and terminfo information, just as every other UNIX
vendor has supplied since time immemorial, including the h19/z19.  Well,
some pinhead engineering manager at DEC decided that they didn't want to
have to worry about "supporting" non-Digital terminals in DEC OSF/1, so
they had the termcap and terminfo entries for non-DEC terminals REMOVED
from DEC OSF/1.  Yes, REMOVED.  You'd think somebody with even half a clue
about what other UNIX vendors supply with their UNIXes would have kept
the functionality in there, but not Digital.  It wasn't even like somebody
"forgot" about putting them in there; no, they deliberately REMOVED
existing, useful functionality from their product.

Digital, get a clue: not every UNIX user has a workstation, and even those
who do have reason on occasion to use a plain, old-fashioned character-cell
terminal.  And you're not the only manufacturer of terminals in the world,
no matter how much you might like to delude yourselves into thinking so...

*flame off*

-Shawn Mamros
E-mail to: mamros@ftp.com
Speaking for myself only...



