In article <CtvMBH.6qL@ucdavis.edu>, mahesh@kale.ece.ucdavis.edu (Mahesh Ramachandran) writes:
|> When I try to use "Atom" on gcc compiled code, "Atom" crashes.
|> It, however, works fine with cc compiled code.


I work on the Atom project at DEC.  Atom should work with gcc, although
it has not been tested with that compiler and isn't guaranteed to work.
As long as gcc emits the proper symbol table and relocation information.
I've tried running Atom over a few things compiled with gcc (version 2.5.5)
and it seems to work OK for me.  Are you noticing a problem with every
program compiled with gcc or only with a particular program?

Gcc can be configured in one of two ways an Alphas.  One way causes gcc
to emit the symbol table in the normal ECOFF format used by the other
tools on DEC OSF/1.  The other way causes gcc to emit the symbol table
using BSD-style stabs which are not supported by the DEC OSF/1 tools.
Atom will not work if gcc is configured to use stabs.  If you are not
sure how gcc is configured, you can attempt to use dbx on an object
compiled with gcc.  If dbx fails horribly, gcc is probably configured to
use stabs, and you will have to reconfigure gcc in order to use Atom.

Also, do you know what versions of OSF/1 and Atom you have.  The OSF/1
version is usually at the top of /etc/motd.  You can get the version of
atom by typing:

	% atom -version


