In article <31r6hf$4e6@sun4.bham.ac.uk>, sjt@xun8.sr.bham.ac.uk (James Tappin) 
writes:
|>
|>This is a fundamental problem in fortran (not just OSF), because a BLOCK 
|>DATA is never explicitly accessed it won't be picked up from the library 
|>unless there is a way of forcing a given member to be linked in. I'm 
|>afraid it's long enough since I did any serious fortran that I've never 
|>checked to see if there is a linker option to do that.
--
On  most systems (including OSF/1) you can get a block data subprogram to be 
loaded from a library by declaring the name of the subprogram as EXTERNAL
in some subroutine that is being loaded.

This behaviour isn't specified by the FORTRAN standard but I haven't yet
encountered a system on which it doesn't work (apart from the field test
version of OSF/1!).

