In article <Cu38A7.LFH@ucdavis.edu>, waterstraat@geology.ucdavis.edu (Paul Waterstraat) writes:
|> 
|> I've tried a number of other fortran related libraries, but none
|> seems to have _OtsDivide32.
|> 
|> Any ideas on where to look next, or what library to use?

You need to link with the libots library.  Include -lots on your link line.

If you need to find the library that defines a symbol, you can try this
set of commands:

	% nm -B -o /usr/lib/*.a | fgrep _OtsDivide32 | egrep -v ' U | N | V '

Where you would replace '_OtsDivide32' with the symbol you need to define.
The first column of the output contains the library name.  For example, the
command above gives:

/usr/lib/libots.a:ots_div.o:    0x00000000000218 T _OtsDivide32
/usr/lib/libots.a:ots_div.o:    0x000000000001f4 T _OtsDivide32Overflow
/usr/lib/libots.a:ots_div.o:    0x00000000000088 T _OtsDivide32Unsigned


