We're planning to migrate one of our ULTRIX machines over to OSF/1 2.0 in a few
weeks time.  We've just discovered that we've got a problem with existing users
as the ULTRIX machine is at enhanced security which means that the
passwords are all crypt16 encrypted and so incompatible with OSF/1 which only
uses crypt.  It looks like we're going to have to get everyone to set a new
password on OSF/1 but with greater than 2000 users, this could be something of
a headache.

The current solution is to have a captive account that users can login to and
give their username and old password.  The password can then be crypt16'd and
checked against the ex-ULTRIX version.  If they match then a crypt'd version
could be placed in their passwd entry and their username removed from the
ex-ULTRIX table, (to stop people forever more being able to reset their
password with the old one).

Does anyone have any comments, criticisms or a better solution?  I'd be
grateful if you could mail them to me as I've been warned of an imminent
disaster at our News server and this problem is quite urgent.

