That's the reason of your problems, the file include `main' function
which does f77 real-time library initialisation and call `MAIN__'
which is the name of fortran main program. 

> testit.o -nocount -lUfor -lfor -lFutil -lm -lots -lc 
> ld:
> test.o: main: multiply defined
> Unresolved:
> MAIN__
> 0.0u 0.0s 0:00 60% 0+8k 3+5io 22pf+0w
> fort: Severe: Failed while trying to link.
> *** Exit 1
> Stop.

> Phil

There are two ways to solve the problem:

1. Just link with `cc', but add f77 libraries:
   cc -o test.exe test.o testit.o -lUfor -lfor -lFutil -lm -lots
  Advantage: simplisity, you can link with C++ code as well.
  Disadvantages: Calls iargc(3f), getarg(3f) will not work (they need
  initialisation in `for_main.o').  You should state explicitly fortran
  libraries. THERE IS POSSIBILITY THAT SOME OF FORTRAN I/O WILL NOT
  WORK PROPERLY. I have never seen source for f77 run time library
  so I don't know what kind of initialisation `for_main' make. From
  my own expirience sequential formatted I/O works fine.

2. Let dummy fortran `PROGRAM DUMMY' calls 'SUBROUTINE CMAIN' and 
  rename your main C program from `main' to `cmain_'.
  Advantages: Do f77 run-time initialisation so all fortran code SHOULD 
  works.
  Disadvantages: A hack is a hack: C++ static objects constructors
  are not called. If you are using g++ add `__main()' call in the very
  beginning of `cmain_' to call the constructors. One needs special code
  if you want to handle  command arguments in a usual C way.
 
Here is an example of passing command arguments from fortran to C, it has
two flaws: Don't try to pass an argment more than 100 characters long,
I get my old code and generalized it a little bit so test before use.

----- cut here main.f ----
      program MAIN
      character*100 opts(100)
c
      do i=0,iargc()
        call getarg(i,opts(i+1))
      enddo
c
      call cstart(iargc()+1,opts)
      end
----- cut here cstart.c ----
/*
 * This code gets argc argv from Fortran Main,  process it for C
 * and call cmain_
 */
#ifdef __GNUC__
#define alloca __builtin_alloca
#else
#include <alloca.h>
#endif

lenocc_(char *string, int len);

int
cstart_(int *argc, char *argv, int len)
{
  int i, lenocc;
  char **av = (char **)alloca(sizeof(char*) * (*argc+1));

  for (i=0; i < *argc; i++) {
    if ((lenocc=lenocc_(&(argv[i*len]),len)) == len) {
      av[i] = alloca(lenocc+1);
      strncpy(av[i],&(argv[i*len]),len);
      av[i][len+1] = '\0';
    } else {
      av[i] = &(argv[i*len]);
      av[i][lenocc] = '\0';
    }
  }
  av[i] = (char*)0;
 
#ifdef __GNUC__
  __main()
#endif
  return cmain_(*argc,av);
}

/*
 * This function returns the actual lenght of Fortran string.
 * (trailing spaces are skipped).
 *
 * There is straighforward and slow implementation.
 */
lenocc_(char *string, int len)
{
  while (string[--len] == ' ');
  return len;
}
